during execution (e.g., via runtime watchers) or only at completion.\n- Cross-check against the published API reference for GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} and GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs.\n- Where feasible, confirm empirically by running an evaluation job against a development instance and inspecting detail-endpoint responses mid-run.\n- Enumerate the message_code values (or equivalent fields) emitted for admission/scheduling/resource-quota failures versus runtime failures, and assess whether they are reliably distinguishable.\n- Publish findings with a per-question go/no-go and recommended fallback for the dependent UI features.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documented answer, with evidence (source references and/or observed API responses), to whether status.benchmarks[] contains per-benchmark status entries while a job is running.\n- Documented enumeration of message_code values for pre-start vs runtime failure paths and a determination of reliable distinguishability.\n- Findings delivered to the ODH Dashboard team with a clear go/no-go per dependent feature (mid-run benchmark progress display; pre-start failure distinction) and recommended fallback when negative.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Benchmark-level progress display (informs feasibility)\n- [P2] Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction (informs feasibility)\n\nHmm — HLR Traceability \"which strategy HLRs this epic covers\". An investigation doesn't cover an HLR per se; it de-risks HLR-4/5/7. I'll phrase as \"de-risks\" — the traceability matrix in the summary maps HLR-4 → E002, E004 (and I can add E001?). The validator checks HLR coverage presumably by... let me check what the validator checks — \"epic body sections, component values, Rule 11, priority inheritance\". It may not check HLR coverage text. I'll list the HLRs the investigation informs, marked as such. To be safe for \"every HLR covered\", main coverage is by E002/E003/E004; E001 informs.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Detail-polling engine for in-progress evaluation jobs\n\n## Description\nDeliver the new detail-polling layer inside the eval-hub-ui dashboard plugin... a React Query based polling hook that identifies non-terminal jobs on the currently displayed Evaluations table page and fetches their detail endpoint (GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}) on a 10-second cycle with at most 5 concurrent requests in round-robin, adopting the autorag package's retry-with-backoff error pattern and pausing while the browser tab is backgrounded. Also verify the odh-dashboard BFF proxy passthrough.\n\n## Scope\n- New detail-polling hook (alongside existing useEvaluationJobs / k8s.ts list fetching) that derives the set of in-progress jobs visible on the current table page (client-side pagination, default 20 rows, options 5/10/20) and polls their detail endpoints.\n- 10-second cycle; when all detail fetches for the current page complete, a 10-second timer starts before the next cycle.\n- Concurrency cap of 5 simultaneous detail requests in round-robin: as one completes the next pending job is fetched, until all in-progress visible jobs are updated.\n- Page changes immediately retarget polling to newly visible jobs; out-of-view jobs are not polled.\n- Error handling per the autorag usePipelineRunQuery pattern: placeholderData preserving last successful response, exponential backoff (RETRY_DELAY_MS * 2^attempt + jitter) up to MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS, 4xx non-retryable, 5xx/network retried; non-blocking PatternFly warning Alert while updates are temporarily unavailable; round-robin continues past a failed request (partial progress data acceptable).\n- Page Visibility API integration: pause polling on backgrounded tab, trigger one immediate cycle on focus.\n- Verification that the odh-dashboard BFF proxy chain transparently passes all detail-endpoint response fields without filtering (no code change expected; validation of existing behavior).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n(1) concurrency/cycle; (2) page retarget; (3) error/backoff/warning; (4) visibility pause; (5) BFF passthrough verified; (6) graceful degradation — detail endpoint unavailable → rows show top-level list state only, existing 30s list polling unregressed.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] Auto-refresh mechanism (detail-polling portion)\n- [P0] Non-blocking error handling during detail polling\n- [P1] Max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch (fetching portion)\n- [P1] Polling pauses when tab backgrounded\n\nE003 body:\n\n# Evaluation job state differentiation in the Evaluations table\n\n## Description\nDeliver visually distinct state treatments... derived from the list endpoint's status.state field, including queued/pending, running, failed, completed (distinct PatternFly icon, color, label), plus the pre-start vs runtime failure distinction where message_code permits, with graceful fallback.\n\n## Scope\n- State mapping from list-endpoint status.state: pending → Queued; active execution → Running; failure → Failed (status.message as context); completed → Completed.\n- Distinct PatternFly Label/Icon/color per state, visually distinguishable.\n- Pre-start vs runtime failure distinction using status.message.message_code where available: admission error/scheduling failure/resource constraint → \"Failed (not started)\" vs \"Failed\" for runtime errors; fallback to a single \"Failed\" state with status.message text when message_code is ambiguous or absent.\n- Auto-updating state badge as list polling refreshes (no manual browser refresh needed).\n- UX/design review of state icon and color specifications for PatternFly alignment.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n(from strategy ACs 2,4,5 + UXD review + fallback)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] Visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, completed\n- [P0] Auto-refresh mechanism (visible state transitions portion)\n- [P2] Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Benchmark progress and elapsed-time display for running evaluation jobs\n\n## Description\nDeliver the progress display... benchmark-level progress (\"Benchmarks: X/N complete\") and elapsed time for running jobs in the Evaluations table, using data from the detail-polling capability, with elapsed time as the primary progress indicator for jobs with few benchmarks.\n\n## Scope\n- Benchmark progress: X = count of status.benchmarks[] entries with status \"completed\", N = total benchmarks → \"Benchmarks: X/N complete\".\n- Elapsed time: current time minus earliest per-benchmark started_at (fallback resource.created_at when no benchmark has started); formatted e.g. \"Elapsed: 4m 12s\".\n- Until the first detail fetch returns for a job, show only top-level state (no progress or elapsed time).\n- Elapsed time as primary progress indicator for single/few-benchmark jobs; benchmark count supplementary.\n- UX/design review of the progress indicator pattern.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n(strategy ACs 1, 3 + initial-state + UXD)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] Auto-refresh mechanism (in-progress job progress portion) — hmm, actually HLR-1 mentions \"new detail endpoint polling for in-progress job progress\". The display of that progress is E004. I'll include a mention.\n- [P1] Benchmark-level progress display (display portion)\n- [P1] Elapsed time display\n\nNow, careful: no sibling epic IDs in bodies. In E004's description I say \"using data from the detail-polling capability\" — good, plain terms. E001 body: \"dependent UI features\" — good. E002: fine. E003: fine.\n\nWait — one issue: E004's dependencies include E002 and gated_by E001. In the body, describe in plain terms: \"Depends on the detail-polling layer having been delivered\" — allowed as plain terms (not epic IDs). Good.\n\nNow write files. Then frontmatter sets:\n\nSummary:\npython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8005-decomposition.md parent_strat=\"RHAISTRAT-8005\" epic_count=4 critical_path_length=3\n\nE001:\nset ... epic_id=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001\" title=\"Validate EvalHub Server mid-run status d