{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-86c9e2c5714222b5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# RHAISTRAT-8005 Decomposition — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n10\t\n11\t## Triage Outcome\n12\t\n13\tThe strategy is S-sized, frontend-only, in a single active component (the eval-hub-ui plugin; the odh-dashboard BFF entry is passive — no code change expected) and owned by a single implementation team (ODH Dashboard). However, the below-threshold path does **not** apply: the strategy explicitly flags two genuine unknowns (Open Questions table, plus \"needs validation\" Assumptions) whose answers change what the downstream UI epics do:\n14\t\n15\t1. Is `status.benchmarks[]` populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is still running, or only after completion?\n16\t2. What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures vs. runtime failures?\n17\t\n18\tBoth require technical work to resolve and both gate downstream scope, so the strategy proceeds to full decomposition with an Investigation epic.\n19\t\n20\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n21\t\n22\tThe Staff Engineer Input section takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach; the following points were adopted verbatim and are reflected in epic scopes:\n23\t\n24\t| # | Staff Engineer direction | Effect on decomposition |\n25\t|---|--------------------------|-------------------------|\n26\t| 1 | Assume no data beyond what the two existing endpoints (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`, `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}`) provide | No server-side epics; all implementation is in the eval-hub-ui plugin; server API extensions stay out of scope |\n27\t| 2 | Poll only jobs visible on the current table page; max 5 parallel fetches, round-robin; 10s cycle; timer starts when all calls complete; scalability deferred for this release | Fixed the polling-engine epic's scope; the earlier open questions on latency window, Page Visibility, and concurrent-user scaling are resolved (no longer unknowns) |\n28\t| 3 | Replicate the autorag/automl React Query retry-with-exponential-backoff pattern; non-blocking warning; last successful status preserved | Adopted as the error-handling approach in the polling-engine epic |\n29\t\n30\t## Epic List\n31\t\n32\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n33\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Validate EvalHub Server mid-run status data availability | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Detail-polling engine for in-progress evaluation jobs | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | Evaluation job state differentiation in the Evaluations table | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 | Benchmark progress and elapsed-time display for running evaluation jobs | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P1 |\n38\t\n39\t## Dependency DAG\n40\t\n41\t```mermaid\n42\tgraph TD\n43\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 Investigation: server mid-run data] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 Detail-polling engine]\n44\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 State differentiation UI]\n45\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 Progress + elapsed-time display]\n46\t    E002 --> E004\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\t## DAG Justification\n50\t\n51\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n52\t|------|------|-----------|\n53\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether `status.benchmarks[]` (and per-benchmark timestamps) change mid-run determines whether a detail-polling layer delivers any mid-run value at all. True gate with `rewrite`: fallback is elapsed-time-only display sourced from the list endpoint's `resource.created_at`, per the strategy's risk mitigation. |\n54\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The `message_code` enumeration determines whether the pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction is implementable. True gate with `rewrite`: fallback is a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text, per the strategy's risk mitigation. |\n55\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Mid-run population of `status.benchmarks[]` determines this epic's scope (benchmark X/N progress mid-run vs. elapsed-time-primary display). True gate with `rewrite`. |\n56\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework/producer → consumer; build-against artifact) | The progress display consumes the detail-polling hook's fetched data and query interface — code in this epic builds against that artifact, so it is serial. |\n57\t| E002 ∥ E003 (no edge) | Rule 14/15 (independent, parallel) | State differentiation renders list-endpoint `status.state` only; it shares no data artifact with the detail-polling engine. Both modify EvaluationsTable row components — a merge-coordination risk, not a dependency. Adding an edge would needlessly serialize P0 state work behind the polling engine. |\n58\t\n59\t**Conditional-branch handling:** each Investigation outcome is bounded (yes/no per question), but the failure mode of every gated epic is a *scope reduction of the same UI surface* with a strategy-defined fallback (documented in each epic's `gate_failure_impact`), not a different epic structure. Branch epics would duplicate the same surfaces, so gates are modeled via `gate_failure_impact` on the main-plan epics instead of separate branch files.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n62\t\n63\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n64\t|-----|----------|---------|\n65\t| Auto-refresh mechanism for the Evaluations landing page (list polling for top-level state + detail polling for in-progress progress) | P0 | E002 (detail-polling layer), E003 (visible state transitions) |\n66\t| Visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, completed states | P0 | E003 |\n67\t| Non-blocking error handling during detail polling (last state preserved, inline warning, exponential backoff) | P0 | E002 |\n68\t| Benchmark-level progress display with max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch | P1 | E002 (fetching), E004 (display) |\n69\t| Elapsed time display for running jobs | P1 | E004 |\n70\t| Polling pauses when tab backgrounded (Page Visibility API) | P1 | E002 |\n71\t| Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via `message_code` | P2 | E003 |\n72\t\n73\tFull HLR coverage: all 7 HLRs map to at least one epic. Priority notes:\n74\t\n75\t- **E002** bundles P0 (HLR auto-refresh/error-handling) with P1 (round-robin fetch, Page Visibility pause): the P1 items modify the same polling hook and fetch cycle — not independently deferrable, so they stay bundled at P0.\n76\t- **E003** bundles P0 (state distinction) with P2 (pre-start failure distinction): both render the same state-badge surface in the table row — per the consolidate rule, a shared-surface P0/P2 pair is one capability, not a deferral signal.\n77\t- **E004** maps to P1 HLRs only (progress display, elapsed time) and blocks no P0 work → P1.\n78\t- **E001** gates P0 work (E002, E003) → implicitly P0 by priority inheritance.\n79\t\n80\t## Component Mapping Notes\n81\t\n82\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Note |\n83\t|--------------------|--------------------------|------|\n84\t| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | AI Core Dashboard | No canonical component named \"eval-hub-ui\" or \"odh-dashboard\"; the plugin lives in the odh-dashboard monorepo, whose closest canonical name is \"AI Core Dashboard\". |\n85\t| odh-dashboard (BFF) | AI Core Dashboard | Passive component — \"no code change expected\", owned by the same team as the active component. Per the passive-component rule, its validation (transparent field passthrough) is an acceptance criterion on E002 rather than a separate epic. |\n86\t| EvalHub Server API (investigation subject) | AI Evaluations | E001 investigates the Eva