{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85a4becc5d544f26","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8005 Decomposition — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Outcome\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy is S-sized, frontend-only, in a single active component (the eval-hub-ui plugin; the odh-dashboard BFF entry is passive — no code change expected) and owned by a single implementation team (ODH Dashboard). However, the below-threshold path does **not** apply: the strategy explicitly flags two genuine unknowns (Open Questions table, plus \"needs validation\" Assumptions) whose answers change what the downstream UI epics do:\n15\t\n16\t1. Is `status.benchmarks[]` populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is still running, or only after completion?\n17\t2. What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures vs. runtime failures?\n18\t\n19\tBoth require technical work to resolve and both gate downstream scope, so the strategy proceeds to full decomposition with an Investigation epic.\n20\t\n21\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n22\t\n23\tThe Staff Engineer Input section takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach; the following points were adopted verbatim and are reflected in epic scopes:\n24\t\n25\t| # | Staff Engineer direction | Effect on decomposition |\n26\t|---|--------------------------|-------------------------|\n27\t| 1 | Assume no data beyond what the two existing endpoints (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`, `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}`) provide | No server-side epics; all implementation is in the eval-hub-ui plugin; server API extensions stay out of scope |\n28\t| 2 | Poll only jobs visible on the current table page; max 5 parallel fetches, round-robin; 10s cycle; timer starts when all calls complete; scalability deferred for this release | Fixed the polling-engine epic's scope; the earlier open questions on latency window, Page Visibility, and concurrent-user scaling are resolved (no longer unknowns) |\n29\t| 3 | Replicate the autorag/automl React Query retry-with-exponential-backoff pattern; non-blocking warning; last successful status preserved | Adopted as the error-handling approach in the polling-engine epic |\n30\t\n31\t## Epic List\n32\t\n33\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n34\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Validate EvalHub Server mid-run status data availability | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Detail-polling engine for in-progress evaluation jobs | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | Evaluation job state differentiation in the Evaluations table | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 | Benchmark progress and elapsed-time display for running evaluation jobs | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P1 |\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 Investigation: server mid-run data] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 Detail-polling engine]\n45\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 State differentiation UI]\n46\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 Progress + elapsed-time display]\n47\t    E002 --> E004\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether `status.benchmarks[]` (and per-benchmark timestamps) change mid-run determines whether a detail-polling layer delivers any mid-run value at all. True gate with `rewrite`: fallback is elapsed-time-only display sourced from the list endpoint's `resource.created_at`, per the strategy's risk mitigation. |\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The `message_code` enumeration determines whether the pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction is implementable. True gate with `rewrite`: fallback is a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text, per the strategy's risk mitigation. |\n56\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Mid-run population of `status.benchmarks[]` determines this epic's scope (benchmark X/N progress mid-run vs. elapsed-time-primary display). True gate with `rewrite`. |\n57\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework/producer → consumer; build-against artifact) | The progress display consumes the detail-polling hook's fetched data and query interface — code in this epic builds against that artifact, so it is serial. |\n58\t| E002 ∥ E003 (no edge) | Rule 14/15 (independent, parallel) | State differentiation renders list-endpoint `status.state` only; it shares no data artifact with the detail-polling engine. Both modify EvaluationsTable row components — a merge-coordination risk, not a dependency. Adding an edge would needlessly serialize P0 state work behind the polling engine. |\n59\t\n60\t**Scheduling note:** the critical path length is 3 (E001 → E002 → E004), above the S-size heuristic of 1–2. This is the structurally necessary consequence of a genuine Investigation gate followed by a producer→consumer chain (the progress display builds against the polling hook's data). E003 (P0) runs parallel to the chain, so only E004 (P1) sits at depth 3. No edge is removable without violating the investigation-gating or build-against rules; plan delivery dates accordingly rather than compressing the DAG.\n61\t\n62\t**Conditional-branch handling:** each Investigation outcome is bounded (yes/no per question), but the failure mode of every gated epic is a *scope reduction of the same UI surface* with a strategy-defined fallback (documented in each epic's `gate_failure_impact`), not a different epic structure. Branch epics would duplicate the same surfaces, so gates are modeled via `gate_failure_impact` on the main-plan epics instead of separate branch files.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n65\t\n66\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n67\t|-----|----------|---------|\n68\t| Auto-refresh mechanism for the Evaluations landing page (list polling for top-level state + detail polling for in-progress progress) | P0 | E002 (detail-polling layer), E003 (visible state transitions) |\n69\t| Visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, completed states | P0 | E003 |\n70\t| Non-blocking error handling during detail polling (last state preserved, inline warning, exponential backoff) | P0 | E002 |\n71\t| Benchmark-level progress display with max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch | P1 | E002 (fetching), E004 (display) |\n72\t| Elapsed time display for running jobs | P1 | E004 |\n73\t| Polling pauses when tab backgrounded (Page Visibility API) | P1 | E002 |\n74\t| Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via `message_code` | P2 | E003 |\n75\t\n76\tFull HLR coverage: all 7 HLRs map to at least one epic. Priority notes:\n77\t\n78\t- **E002** bundles P0 (HLR auto-refresh/error-handling) with P1 (round-robin fetch, Page Visibility pause): the P1 items modify the same polling hook and fetch cycle — not independently deferrable, so they stay bundled at P0.\n79\t- **E003** bundles P0 (state distinction) with P2 (pre-start failure distinction): both render the same state-badge surface in the table row — per the consolidate rule, a shared-surface P0/P2 pair is one capability, not a deferral signal.\n80\t- **E004** maps to P1 HLRs only (progress display, elapsed time) and blocks no P0 work → P1.\n81\t- **E001** gates P0 work (E002, E003) → implicitly P0 by priority inheritance.\n82\t\n83\t## Component Mapping Notes\n84\t\n85\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Note |\n86\t|--------------------|--------------------------|------|\n87\t| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | AI Core Dashboard | No canonical component named \"eval-hub-ui\" or \"odh-dashboard\"; the plugin lives in the odh-dashboard monorepo, whose closest canonical name is \"AI Core Dashboard\". |\n88\t| odh-dashboard (BFF) | AI Core Dashboard | Passive component — \"no code change expected\", owned by the same team as the active component. Per the passive-component rule, its validation (transparent field passthrough) is an acceptance criterion on E002 rather than a separate epic. |\n89\t| EvalHub Server API (investigation subject) | AI Evaluations | E001 investigates the EvalHub Server's status-reporting behavior; closest canonical name for the evaluations service. |\n90\t\n91\t## Health Warnings\n92\t\n93\t- **Proposed ACs**: the strategy's acceptance criteria are explicitly marked \"Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation\"; PM sign-off is outstanding.\n94\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all listed \"Pending human review\". These are strategy gates, not modeled as epics; UXD review is captured as an acceptance criterion on the two UI epics.\n95\t- **Coarse progress granularity**: single-benchmark jobs show binary benchmark progress (0/N → N/N). Mitigated in-plan by making elapsed time the primary progress indicator; UX acceptability should be confirmed by UXD review.\n96\t- **`message_code` reliability risk**: the strategy itself flags that `message_code` values may not consistently distinguish pre-start from runtime failures across all scenarios; the fallback (single \"Failed\" state with message text) is built into E003's scope.\n97\t- **No docs-authoring epic**: Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" with no substantive doc topics in scope; the \"progress indicator UX specification\" design doc is a UX artifact folded into the UI epics' review criteria, not Red Hat docs content.\n98\t\n99\t## Tiered Delivery\n100\t\n101\tNot applicable. All dependencies (internal plugin infrastructure, BFF proxy routing, both EvalHub Server API endpoints, PatternFly, React Query) are status \"Exists\"; there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The only gate is the internal Investigation (E001), handled via `gate_failure_impact` fallbacks as described above.\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87740d0a423e0d67","agent_id":"a7ba9555a12ae6a06","session_id":"2253a725-5f5e-46df-9ff1-988eb7749e1b","uuid":"c1202612-4ffc-495b-9d53-cf1ee923d7b7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:15:35.801Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-85a4becc5d544f26","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
